{
  "skillId": "simplify-fractions-by-factorising",
  "atomType": "R",
  "theory": {
    "intro": "An algebraic fraction can be simplified only when its numerator and denominator share a common **factor**. Factorising both parts first makes those common factors visible so they can be cancelled.",
    "facts": [
      "$\\frac{A}{B}$ can be simplified **if and only if** $A$ and $B$ share a common factor other than $1$.",
      "Only **factors** cancel, never single **terms**: $\\frac{x+3}{x+5}$ cannot be simplified, because $x$ is a term of each part, not a factor of it.",
      "The factorising tools are the common factor ($6x+8=2(3x+4)$), the difference of two squares ($x^2-25=(x-5)(x+5)$) and trinomials ($x^2+5x+6=(x+2)(x+3)$).",
      "A numerator and denominator that are **negatives** of each other cancel to $-1$: $3-x=-(x-3)$, so $\\frac{3-x}{x-3}=-1$.",
      "To **multiply**, factorise everything and cancel a factor above against the same factor below; to **divide**, multiply by the reciprocal first.",
      "To **add or subtract**, factorise the denominators first, then rewrite both fractions over the lowest common denominator. Every numerator carries invisible brackets: $\\frac{a-b}{c}$ means $\\frac{(a-b)}{c}$.",
      "If no common factor appears after factorising, the fraction is already in simplest form."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Factorise the numerator and denominator.",
      "Cancel any common factors."
    ]
  },
