      {
        "question_text": "Kim writes $\\frac{x+7}{x+2}=\\frac{7}{2}$ by \"cancelling the $x$\". Explain why this is wrong, using $x=3$ to check.",
        "structure": "identify-simplest-form",
        "solution_text": "In $\\frac{x+7}{x+2}$ the $x$ is a **term** of each part, not a factor of it, so it cannot be cancelled.\n$x=3$: $\\frac{3+7}{3+2}=\\frac{10}{5}=2$\n$\\frac{7}{2}=3.5$\nThe two values disagree, so the cancelling is invalid."
      }
